---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/business/intermediate/operations-and-supply-chains">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Operations turns plans into delivered products — <strong>managing flow through constrained
    systems under uncertainty</strong>. It is where strategy meets physics: the bottleneck maths,
    the inventory trade-offs and the supply-chain dynamics decide whether the business model can
    actually execute.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="constraints, buffers, bullwhip, and built-in quality" widget="equationStep" config={opsCfg} />

  <h2>Supply chains, honestly understood</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Just-in-time vs just-in-case:</strong> lean inventory cut costs for decades — then COVID and the Suez blockage showed the fragility. Resilience (buffer stock, dual suppliers) is insurance with a premium.</li>
    <li><strong>Outsourcing decisions:</strong> make-or-buy is about control, capability and coordination cost — not just unit price. Hidden coordination costs are why "simple" outsourcing projects overrun.</li>
    <li><strong>Queueing is everywhere:</strong> utilisation above ~80% makes waiting times explode nonlinearly (queueing theory). Same maths runs hospital ERs, call centres and CPU schedulers — overloading a system degrades it disproportionately.</li>
    <li><strong>Measurement drives behaviour:</strong> every operational metric gamed is a metric misdesigned (Goodhart's law). Choose metrics that can't be gamed without doing the real work.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Goldratt, The Goal; Womack & Jones, Lean Thinking; Simchi-Levi, Designing and Managing the Supply Chain.</p>
</TopicPage>
